// The goto table defaults per nonterminal rather than per state: the state a goto on a nonterminal leads to in most of
// the states which have such a goto is taken out of the table, and only the states which disagree keep an entry. A
// lookup therefore answers out of two different places depending on which state asks, and this case makes a parse walk
// through both.
//
// The same sum is reduced inside three different brackets, so the gotos on it lead to three different states. One of
// them is the default and the other two are entries of their own, which means the trace only comes out right if the
// lookup consults the row before it falls back to the default. A backend which reads the default first parses the first
// bracket correctly and continues in the wrong state for the other two, so the input has to close every bracket it
// opens to catch that: the closing bracket is the token which only the correct state accepts.
//
// The sum is recursive so that a goto happens more than once per bracket, and the brackets are nested inside a list so
// that the gotos on the surrounding nonterminals are exercised as well.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER:   /[0-9]+/;
    PLUS:     "+";

    LPAREN:   "(";
    RPAREN:   ")";
    LBRACKET: "[";
    RBRACKET: "]";
    LBRACE:   "{";
    RBRACE:   "}";
}

@parser {
    groups
        : groups group
        | group
        ;

    group
        : "(" sum ")"
        | "[" sum "]"
        | "{" sum "}"
        ;

    sum
        : NUMBER
        | sum "+" NUMBER
        ;
}
